=== L6 Specialist Writing on Film: Reviews, Theory, Cinematography ===
id: 71721fb1-b50e-47ee-8fa5-66774b7fc8a1
slug: specialist-writing-on-film-reviews-theory-cinematography
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:37:01.224447+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How to read and use a film review, a piece of film criticism and writing on cinematography.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Specialist Writing Matters for This Unit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The extended-writing question on the US independent film includes a source of specialist writing: a piece of criticism, a film review, or an extract of writing on <dfn class="term" data-def="The art and practice of photographing motion pictures; encompasses shot type, camera movement, lens choice, lighting design and framing, all considered as deliberate meaning-making choices.">cinematography</dfn>. Your task is not to summarise or paraphrase that source &mdash; it is to <em>use</em> it as a tool in your own argument about a specific named sequence of your chosen film. This lesson teaches the skill of application, not the content of any particular source. The actual sources rotate every three years and are provided by your teacher; this platform teaches you how to engage with any source you are given.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what are the three main types of specialist writing you might be given as a source, and what does each type primarily do?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Three types of specialist writing you may encounter: (1) a <strong>film review</strong> &mdash; evaluates a specific film on its release, combining description, analysis and evaluative judgement; (2) a piece of <strong>film criticism</strong> &mdash; develops an interpretive argument about a film or filmmaker, typically longer and more analytical than a review; (3) writing on <strong>cinematography</strong> &mdash; analyses how a film&rsquo;s visual choices create meaning, often focusing on technique and its effects.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Three Types of Specialist Writing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A piece of writing that evaluates a film on or near its release, typically combining plot summary, description of key sequences, analysis of performances and direction, and an overall evaluative judgement aimed at a general audience.">film review</dfn> is written for a general educated audience, close to the film&rsquo;s release. Its primary purpose is evaluative: the reviewer tells the reader whether the film is worth their time and why. Writers like Pauline Kael (the New Yorker), Roger Ebert (the Chicago Sun-Times) and Mark Kermode (the Observer) are among the most widely referenced critics students may encounter. Their reviews are characterised by vivid scene description, strong evaluative judgements, and specialist vocabulary used accessibly. When you apply a review to your analysis, you treat it as evidence for a claim, not as a summary to repeat.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Interpretive writing about film that develops an argument or reading of a film, filmmaker or trend, typically longer and more theoretically grounded than a review, aimed at an informed or academic readership.">Film criticism</dfn> is more analytical in register than a review. It develops an argument &mdash; a thesis about what a film is doing, how it is doing it, and why that matters &mdash; and supports that argument through close reading of sequences, reference to production context, and sometimes film theory. Academic film writing, associated with critics and scholars such as David Bordwell, uses this mode: precise, evidence-driven, arguing a specific case about film form and meaning.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n7">Writing on <dfn class="term" data-def="Specialist writing that analyses the technical and aesthetic choices a director of photography makes &mdash; lens choices, lighting design, camera movement &mdash; and argues for their meaning in the context of a specific film.">cinematography writing</dfn> is the most technically focused of the three types. It asks: what specific choices did the director of photography make, and what effect do those choices have on the spectator&rsquo;s experience of a specific sequence? This type of writing often reads like applied micro-analysis: it names a shot type, describes the lighting state, identifies the camera movement, and then explains the meaning that combination generates. For your extended-writing question, this type of source is an invitation to do the same in your own argument.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">The Application Skill: Three Steps</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Regardless of which type of source you receive, the method of application is the same. There are three steps:</p>

<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Step 1: Identify the source&rsquo;s argument.</strong> What is the writer&rsquo;s central claim? Not &ldquo;the writer talks about&rdquo; but &ldquo;the writer argues that&rdquo;. A review might argue that a director uses close-ups in an unusually intimate way; a piece of criticism might argue that a film&rsquo;s editing rhythm enacts its protagonist&rsquo;s psychological state; a piece on cinematography might argue that a specific lighting choice positions the spectator as morally implicated. Name the claim precisely.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Step 2: Quote a short, precise fragment.</strong> Five to ten words from the source is enough. The fragment should capture the writer&rsquo;s core claim or their most precise language about the film. You are not retelling the source &mdash; you are taking a piece of its argument to use as a lens. Introduce it with the writer&rsquo;s name: &ldquo;[Writer] argues that...&rdquo; or &ldquo;[Writer] describes the sequence as...&rdquo;</p>

<p data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Step 3: Apply the argument to a named sequence.</strong> Now use the source&rsquo;s idea to open up your own analysis of a specific moment in your chosen film. The source is a key &mdash; it unlocks a reading of a sequence you already know. Imagine a critic writes: &ldquo;Gerwig directs intimacy the way other directors direct spectacle.&rdquo; You would apply that idea to the closing voicemail-and-walk sequence in Lady Bird by showing <em>how</em> the tracking shot, the cross-cutting and the warm-to-cool palette shift together construct intimacy across distance &mdash; your analysis, using the critic&rsquo;s frame as the starting point.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Applying a Fictional Critical Source to Whiplash</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Imagine a critic of US independent cinema writes: &ldquo;Chazelle directs sound the way other directors direct light.&rdquo; This is a fictional example &mdash; do not use it as a real quotation in your answer, but practise the application method with it.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Step 1 &mdash; Identify the argument: the writer argues that Chazelle treats sound as his primary aesthetic tool, using it with the intentionality and precision that a visually focused director might use lighting. Step 2 &mdash; Fragment quoted: &ldquo;directs sound the way other directors direct light.&rdquo; Step 3 &mdash; Application: &ldquo;This idea illuminates the closing-solo sequence, in which the diegetic percussion moves from a controlled, metronomic rhythm to an accelerating, almost arrhythmic texture. The non-diegetic score drops out completely; what the spectator hears is entirely produced within the scene. The sound design constructs the spectator&rsquo;s alignment with the protagonist&rsquo;s physical and psychological state: the closer the drumming comes to breaking the tempo, the more the spectator is positioned inside the ambition Chazelle is analysing. The critic&rsquo;s claim is confirmed by the sequence: the sound is the film&rsquo;s visual language.&rdquo;</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Notice: the source fragment is used once, introduced by the writer&rsquo;s name, then left behind as the student&rsquo;s own analysis takes over. The source is a door, not a room.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write out the three-step application method in your own words. Then apply it to one sentence from any review of a film you know.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The three-step application method: (1) identify the source&rsquo;s argument in your own words; (2) quote a precise 5&ndash;10-word fragment, introduced by the writer&rsquo;s name; (3) use the argument as a lens to open up your own analysis of a named sequence in the chosen film. The source does not replace your analysis &mdash; it enables it.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Auteur Theory as a Critical Lens</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18"><dfn class="term" data-def="A critical approach, developed by French critics including Fran&#231;ois Truffaut and later systematised by Andrew Sarris, that argues a film's director is its primary creative author, whose consistent formal and thematic choices can be read as a personal vision across their body of work.">Auteur theory</dfn> originated with the French New Wave critics of the 1950s, most influentially Fran&ccedil;ois Truffaut, and was systematised in English by the American critic Andrew Sarris. Its core claim is that certain directors impose a recognisable style and set of concerns across their films, such that their work can be read as an oeuvre &mdash; a body of work with consistent authorial fingerprints. At GCSE level, you use auteur theory as a lens for understanding why a director&rsquo;s formal choices are consistent and interpretable, not as a biographical claim about their intentions.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n19">Applied to the US independent films in this unit, auteur theory is useful when you can point to specific, repeated formal choices: Gerwig&rsquo;s persistent two-shot as a signature; Bigelow&rsquo;s handheld-and-telephoto combination as a consistent aesthetic strategy; Tillman&rsquo;s systematic palette coding. The theory supports your analysis when you can say &ldquo;this choice is characteristic of this director across their work&rdquo; and name the formal evidence.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What Makes Good Specialist Vocabulary in Extended Writing?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Specialist vocabulary in an extended-writing answer is vocabulary used <em>precisely</em>, not just mentioned. The difference: writing &ldquo;the film uses <dfn class="term" data-def="Editing in which the cuts and camera positions follow consistent spatial rules that allow the spectator to orient themselves within a scene, maintaining the illusion of a continuous, coherent space.">continuity editing</dfn>&rdquo; earns nothing if the rest of the sentence does not explain what that editing choice does in this sequence. Writing &ldquo;the continuity editing in the 'the talk' scene creates a seamless domestic space that the rest of the film will gradually fragment&rdquo; uses the term to make an argument. Aim to use every specialist term you introduce as part of a claim about meaning, not as a catalogue of techniques spotted.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21">Strong specialist vocabulary for the US independent unit: <dfn class="term" data-def="The aesthetic look and feel of a film considered as a whole, including its colour palette, camera style, sound design and production design, which together give the film its distinctive visual identity.">visual aesthetic</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="A form of cinema associated with very low budgets, naturalistic performance styles, improvised or semi-improvised dialogue, and an intimate, small-scale approach to storytelling, particularly associated with US indie cinema of the 2000s.">mumblecore</dfn> (used where appropriate to contrast), <dfn class="term" data-def="An international independent film festival circuit including Sundance, SXSW and Tribeca that functions as the primary marketplace and prestige platform for US and international independent cinema.">festival circuit</dfn>, institutional context, specialty division, and &mdash; for the criticism lesson specifically &mdash; evaluative judgement, interpretive argument and close reading.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Three types of specialist writing you may be given: a film review (evaluative, general audience), film criticism (interpretive argument, analytical register) and writing on cinematography (technical analysis of visual choices and their effects).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">The three-step application method: identify the source&rsquo;s argument; quote a precise 5&ndash;10 word fragment with the writer&rsquo;s name; apply the argument as a lens to a named sequence in your chosen film using your own micro-analysis.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Auteur theory is a useful critical lens for the US independent unit &mdash; point to specific repeated formal choices across a director&rsquo;s work as evidence, not just to a biographical claim about intention.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">For the extended-writing question that involves a source, the most common error is summarising what the critic says rather than using their argument to support your own analysis. The mark scheme rewards AO2 (your analysis of the film) more heavily than AO1 (your knowledge of the source), so most of your answer should be your close reading of a named sequence, with the source fragment deployed once as a launching point. Keep the quotation short &mdash; five to ten words &mdash; introduce it with the writer&rsquo;s name, then move immediately into your own micro-analysis. A second common error is applying the source to the whole film rather than to a specific named sequence: the mark scheme consistently rewards anchor-to-a-moment over general thematic claims. For an 8-mark question on how specialist writing analyses cinematography, structure your answer as: (1) what the writer&rsquo;s argument is, in one sentence; (2) one quoted fragment; (3) your application of that argument to a named sequence, with at least two micro-features linked to their effect on the spectator.</p>

